1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,257
Hello and welcome back to JD Science This

2
00:00:04,273 --> 00:00:07,779
is part two of our Level 3 lesson on the transmission of a

3
00:00:07,794 --> 00:00:11,498
nerve impulse In part one we left the

4
00:00:11,513 --> 00:00:16,437
axon polarised at about minus seventy millivolts Now

5
00:00:16,452 --> 00:00:20,279
we will watch that membrane reverse and recover and we will draw

6
00:00:20,294 --> 00:00:24,623
the action-potential graph as each event happens A

7
00:00:24,638 --> 00:00:28,175
stimulus is any change that can affect the membrane such as a

8
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:32,291
neurotransmitter arriving at a dendrite or an artificial

9
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:36,071
current in an experiment If the stimulus is

10
00:00:36,087 --> 00:00:39,867
strong enough the membrane potential at one region moves

11
00:00:39,882 --> 00:00:43,678
from minus seventy millivolts towards a threshold value of about

12
00:00:43,693 --> 00:00:47,443
minus fifty-five millivolts Threshold

13
00:00:47,459 --> 00:00:51,132
is the critical voltage at which voltage-gated sodium channels

14
00:00:51,148 --> 00:00:54,791
open in large numbers Voltage-gated

15
00:00:54,806 --> 00:00:59,699
means the channel's shape depends on the membrane potential Below

16
00:00:59,715 --> 00:01:03,526
threshold too few of those channels open the sodium

17
00:01:03,541 --> 00:01:07,520
that enters is pumped or leaks away and the membrane returns

18
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:11,437
to rest That failed rise is not an action

19
00:01:11,452 --> 00:01:15,126
potential If threshold is reached the

20
00:01:15,141 --> 00:01:19,242
response is all-or-nothing The action potential

21
00:01:19,257 --> 00:01:22,901
always reaches about the same peak around plus thirty

22
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:27,108
to plus forty millivolts A stronger stimulus

23
00:01:27,123 --> 00:01:30,782
does not make a taller spike It can only

24
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:34,501
make action potentials more frequent once the membrane

25
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:38,876
has recovered Look at the graph The

26
00:01:38,891 --> 00:01:43,205
horizontal axis is time in milliseconds The

27
00:01:43,221 --> 00:01:48,388
vertical axis is membrane potential in millivolts Zero

28
00:01:48,404 --> 00:01:52,443
millivolts is drawn as a dashed line Resting

29
00:01:52,459 --> 00:01:56,574
potential sits near minus seventy Threshold

30
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:00,263
is marked near minus fifty-five The

31
00:02:00,279 --> 00:02:04,806
peak is near plus thirty-five Depolarisation

32
00:02:04,821 --> 00:02:08,449
is the rising phase Voltage-gated

33
00:02:08,465 --> 00:02:12,215
sodium channels open and sodium ions rush in down their

34
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:15,873
electrochemical gradient The inside

35
00:02:15,888 --> 00:02:20,416
rapidly becomes less negative then positive The

36
00:02:20,431 --> 00:02:24,120
membrane is now depolarised On

37
00:02:24,135 --> 00:02:28,129
the graph this is the steep upstroke from threshold to the peak

38
00:02:29,181 --> 00:02:32,977
At the peak two things change The

39
00:02:32,992 --> 00:02:38,327
voltage-gated sodium channels become inactivated Inactivation

40
00:02:38,343 --> 00:02:42,154
is not the same as simply closing A part

41
00:02:42,169 --> 00:02:46,178
of the protein blocks the open pore from the inside so sodium

42
00:02:46,193 --> 00:02:50,797
entry stops even though the membrane is still positive At

43
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:54,638
the same time voltage-gated potassium channels open

44
00:02:55,690 --> 00:03:00,934
They are slower to open than the sodium channels Repolarisation

45
00:03:00,949 --> 00:03:04,791
is the falling phase Potassium ions

46
00:03:04,806 --> 00:03:08,510
leave the axon through those open voltage-gated potassium

47
00:03:08,526 --> 00:03:12,245
channels Positive charge leaves so the

48
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:16,285
inside becomes negative again This rapid

49
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:20,644
fall is caused by potassium efflux and sodium inactivation

50
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:26,285
It is not caused by the sodium potassium pump The

51
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:30,492
pump is far too slow to produce this millisecond downstroke

52
00:03:31,544 --> 00:03:35,507
The pump's job is to restore the ion gradients afterwards

53
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:41,467
The membrane often goes a little more negative than rest That

54
00:03:41,482 --> 00:03:46,818
undershoot is hyperpolarisation sometimes called after-hyperpolarisation

55
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:51,559
It happens because voltage-gated potassium channels are still

56
00:03:51,574 --> 00:03:55,400
open for a short time As they close

57
00:03:55,583 --> 00:03:59,333
and as leak channels and the pump continue their resting work the

58
00:03:59,348 --> 00:04:04,272
potential returns towards minus seventy millivolts Follow

59
00:04:04,287 --> 00:04:08,403
the graph once more Rest at minus seventy

60
00:04:09,455 --> 00:04:14,196
A stimulus lifts the potential to threshold Sodium

61
00:04:14,211 --> 00:04:19,424
enters and the trace shoots to about plus thirty-five Sodium

62
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:23,281
channels inactivate potassium leaves and the trace

63
00:04:23,296 --> 00:04:27,260
falls A brief dip below minus seventy

64
00:04:27,443 --> 00:04:31,223
then a return to rest The whole event lasts

65
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:36,132
about two to four milliseconds in a typical axon Here

66
00:04:36,147 --> 00:04:40,095
is a worked examination-style example The

67
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:44,577
graph is at plus thirty-five millivolts Describe

68
00:04:44,592 --> 00:04:48,357
the state of the voltage-gated sodium channels and the voltage-gated

69
00:04:48,373 --> 00:04:53,022
potassium channels and name the phase that follows At

70
00:04:53,037 --> 00:04:56,757
the peak voltage-gated sodium channels are inactivated

71
00:04:57,031 --> 00:05:01,147
so sodium can no longer enter Voltage-gated

72
00:05:01,162 --> 00:05:05,004
potassium channels are open so potassium is leaving

73
00:05:06,056 --> 00:05:10,049
The phase that follows is repolarisation as the inside

74
00:05:10,065 --> 00:05:13,998
becomes negative again Now pause the video

75
00:05:14,013 --> 00:05:17,702
and try this A student says the

76
00:05:17,717 --> 00:05:21,467
sodium potassium pump causes the falling phase of the action

77
00:05:21,482 --> 00:05:25,735
potential by pumping sodium out Identify

78
00:05:25,751 --> 00:05:30,308
the misconception and write a corrected sentence Here

79
00:05:30,324 --> 00:05:34,272
is a model answer That statement is wrong

80
00:05:35,324 --> 00:05:39,394
The falling phase is repolarisation caused by potassium

81
00:05:39,409 --> 00:05:43,068
ions leaving through voltage-gated potassium channels after

82
00:05:43,083 --> 00:05:46,757
the sodium channels inactivate The pump

83
00:05:46,772 --> 00:05:50,400
later restores the sodium and potassium gradients but

84
00:05:50,415 --> 00:05:54,333
it does not produce the rapid downstroke In

85
00:05:54,348 --> 00:05:58,220
part three we will see how this local reversal starts the next

86
